Angioedema
Dermatology & Soft Tissue
Sudden subcutaneous swelling, Involvement of lips/face/tongue, Sensation of painful tension/burning

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DST-006
Reason for Visit: Acute, rapidly progressive facial swelling with associated painful tension, raising concern for impending airway compromise.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, Partner
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage documented sudden onset of pronounced swelling involving the highly distensible tissues of the face and lips, progressing over 2 hours. Urgent assessment was required to evaluate for inspiratory stridor, laryngeal oedema, and immediate anaphylaxis risk, warranting intervention prior to the next in-hours period to prevent fatal airway obstruction.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My face feels like it's going to burst, and my lips are so huge and tight they are throbbing."
History of Present Illness: The patient was entirely well until 4 hours ago when they noticed a mild aching sensation in the lower lip. Over the subsequent 2 hours, there was sudden onset pronounced swelling of the deep dermis and subcutaneous tissues, progressing to severe, asymmetrical involvement of the lips, eyelids, and right cheek. The patient describes a sensation of painful tension and burning rather than intense pruritus. They deny superficial wheals, severe abdominal cramping, or subjective shortness of breath. The patient recently commenced an ACE inhibitor for hypertension 10 days prior. The rapid progression prompted the urgent call.
Functional Status: Independent, currently experiencing extreme physical discomfort and anxiety.
Social/Safety Concerns: Patient lives 30 minutes from the nearest emergency department; high risk of acute airway decompensation in transit if unmanaged.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/88 | HR: 104 | Temp: 36.9 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Visibly distressed, sitting upright, speaking in full sentences but articulation is muffled due to labial swelling.
Physical Exam: Marked, asymmetrical, non-pitting edema of the upper and lower lips, extending to the right periorbital and buccal regions. The overlying skin exhibits normal colour with slight erythema, completely lacking superficial wheals (urticaria). The swelling has a firm, tense quality on palpation. Oropharyngeal examination reveals normal uvula, soft palate, and posterior pharynx without evident edema. Auscultation demonstrates clear breath sounds bilaterally; no inspiratory stridor or wheeze.
Environmental Observations: Clean home, functional phone access.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient recently started Perindopril 5mg daily.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: ACE Inhibitor-Induced Angioedema - Status: Acute Exacerbation
Differentials:

Anaphylaxis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of systemic involvement such as hypotension, wheeze, generalized urticaria, or gastrointestinal distress.

Facial Cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Hyper-acute onset (hours, not days), absence of fever, and lack of a demarcated, spreading, hot erythematous plaque.

Contact Dermatitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Lack of intense pruritus, weeping, or superficial epidermal changes; swelling is deep and subcutaneous.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (due to distance to hospital if airway deteriorates)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, pronounced, asymmetric swelling of the facial and labial tissues, strongly characteristic of isolated bradykinin-mediated angioedema secondary to recent ACE inhibitor initiation. The absence of urticaria, hypotension, and stridor currently rules out immediate anaphylaxis, but the rapid progression of the swelling in the preceding hours posed a substantial risk of laryngeal involvement. Telephone triage correctly identified the potential for sudden airway occlusion. Urgent on-site assessment was mandatory to physically inspect the oropharynx, confirm airway patency, immediately cease the offending medication, and formulate a high-acuity safety-netting plan. Delaying assessment until the morning carried an unacceptable risk of asphyxiation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediately cease Perindopril. Administered stat dose of oral Prednisolone 50mg and Cetirizine 10mg (recognizing limited efficacy in bradykinin-mediated cases but providing a safety buffer for mixed presentations).
Positive Behavioral Change: By completely avoiding all ACE inhibitor medications forever, you will prevent this life-threatening swelling from ever happening again. By sleeping propped up on three pillows tonight, you will use gravity to drain the fluid away from your face, which will reduce the painful pressure and make breathing easier.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not required.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not required.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review tomorrow morning for blood pressure management with an alternative class (e.g., Calcium Channel Blocker) and clinical re-evaluation of the swelling.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If you experience any change in your voice, a feeling of a "lump" in your throat, difficulty swallowing your saliva, or any noisy, high-pitched breathing, you must use an EpiPen (if available) and call 000 immediately for an ambulance. Do not attempt to drive.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Angioedema can be unpredictable. While your airway is currently clear, swelling can occasionally shift or worsen over the first 24-48 hours.
Clinical Course: The swelling is transient and typically resolves completely within 72 hours once the medication is stopped, though it may take longer to completely clear the drug from your system.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Continuous observation by your partner overnight is mandatory. Seek immediate emergency care at the first sign of voice change or swallowing difficulty.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The blood pressure medication you started caused a chemical build-up in your tissues, leading to this sudden fluid leak. Stopping the drug is the primary cure.
Actionable Steps: Never take Perindopril or any related "-pril" drug again. Ensure your partner knows the warning signs of throat swelling to call an ambulance instantly.

